  "narrative": "The twelve new results test whether the five major dataset-specific associations persist in adherent culture, the KY CRISPR library, and screen_batch=batch_a, and they also test two focused MDM2/MYC/TP53 questions. The sign convention must be read carefully: for direction -1 outcomes, positive signed estimates correspond to raw more-negative dependency scores, i.e. stronger dependency in the exposed group; for direction +1 outcomes, positive signed estimates correspond to raw higher scores, i.e. weaker dependency in the exposed group.\n\nThe major associations remain robust in the important technical and culture subsets. SMARCA4 loss remains associated with very large stronger SMARCA2 dependency within batch_a (H96: +0.747, 95% CI +0.709 to +0.785). KEAP1 loss remains associated with very large weaker NFE2L2 dependency both within the KY library (H93: +0.794, +0.752 to +0.837) and within batch_a (H97: +0.812, +0.765 to +0.858). MSI-high remains associated with very large stronger WRN dependency within adherent culture (H83: +0.813, +0.780 to +0.846), the KY library (H95: +0.732, +0.675 to +0.790), and batch_a (H98: +0.824, +0.766 to +0.882). PTEN loss remains associated with stronger STAT3 dependency within adherent culture (H82: +0.262, +0.203 to +0.321) and batch_a (H99: +0.234, +0.125 to +0.342). TP53 loss remains associated with weaker USP7 dependency within batch_a (H100: +0.417, +0.284 to +0.550). These are not causal claims or cross-batch interaction claims; they are subset associations showing that the previously validated main effects are not confined to the dominant library, a single screen quality stratum, or one co-alteration context. Conditioning on batch, culture, or library could in principle introduce collider/selection effects, but the estimates are large and intervals are far from zero, so the most parsimonious dataset-specific reading is robustness rather than artifact.\n\nThe only accepted subset effect with material uncertainty is H94: PTEN loss versus PTEN wild-type in the KY library for dependency_STAT3, +0.158, 95% CI +0.063 to +0.254, n=64 exposed. It is directionally consistent and its lower confidence bound still exceeds a practically meaningful scale relative to the STAT3 score, but it is the smallest and one of the least stable accepted subset estimates. I therefore accept it on current discovery evidence as a dataset-specific supportive subset test, but request independent validation to determine whether the PTEN-loss/STAT3 association survives this technical library context with a validation-adjusted interval.\n\nThe MDM2 tests do not currently support the planned mechanism hypothesis. H79, MYC amplification versus no MYC amplification among TP53-wild-type lines for dependency_MDM2, has a tiny signed estimate (+0.008, 95% CI -0.023 to +0.040), with the confidence interval crossing zero. H101, the MYC-amplification x TP53-loss interaction for dependency_MDM2, is also near null (+0.004, -0.071 to +0.078). Because their upper confidence bounds are not small enough to exclude a modest positive effect, I leave them unresolved rather than accepting or formally refuting a moderate interaction. However, they are not high priority for further investigation now, because the point estimates are close to zero and the existing MDM2 TP53-loss main effect was also null. The main conclusions from this update are that the five large validated associations are internally robust across adherent culture, KY-library screens, and the largest screen batch; the MDM2 MYC/TP53 follow-up is not supported by current evidence.",
  "research_notes": "Round 7 appraise update. Accepted new robustness subset tests: H82 (PTEN-loss/STAT3, adherent), H83 (MSI/WRN, adherent), H93 (KEAP1/NFE2L2, KY), H95 (MSI/WRN, KY), H96 (SMARCA4/SMARCA2, batch_a), H97 (KEAP1/NFE2L2, batch_a), H98 (MSI/WRN, batch_a), H99 (PTEN-loss/STAT3, batch_a), H100 (TP53-loss/USP7, batch_a). H94 (PTEN-loss/STAT3, KY) is accepted on discovery evidence but remains active because it is the smallest accepted subset effect; requested validation. H79 (MYC effect on MDM2 in TP53-WT) and H101 (MYC x TP53-loss interaction for MDM2) are near null with intervals crossing zero and upper bounds not tight enough to exclude modest effects; left unresolved/deferred and low priority. Remaining major associations now have unusually broad internal robustness: SMARCA4/SMARCA2, KEAP1/NFE2L2, MSI/WRN, PTEN/STAT3, TP53/USP7 remain supported across adherent culture, KY library, screen quality, co-alteration subsets, and batch_a. These remain associations in this supplied dataset only, not causal or generalized claims. If H94 validation fails, restrict the PTEN/STAT3 association to the main and non-KY contexts rather than invalidating the overall validated effect."
